index 000000000..b729c8b3b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/infra/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,125 @@
+# infra/ — declarative device provisioning, every OS the estate trusts
+
+This directory is the **install surface** for every device the estate trusts: a founder's
+Mac, a CI runner on Ubuntu, a dev container on Debian, an enterprise customer's laptop.
+One orchestrator script, per-OS install artifacts, identical status contract.
+
+## What's here
+
+```
+infra/
+├── macos/                              brew + launchd install on macOS
+│   ├── Brewfile                        pinned formulae + casks
+│   ├── LaunchDaemons/
+│   │   ├── com.idp.spire-agent.plist   user LaunchDaemon
+│   │   ├── com.idp.executor.plist      root LaunchDaemon
+│   │   └── com.estate.jit-device-renew.plist
+│   └── spire-agent.conf.template       rendered to /etc/spire/agent.conf at install
+└── linux/                              apt + systemd install on Debian/Ubuntu
+    ├── apt-lists/
+    │   └── runtime.txt                 apt-get install input
+    └── systemd-units/
+        ├── spire-agent.service         user systemd unit
+        ├── jit-device-renew.service    user systemd unit
+        ├── jit-device-renew.timer      every-10-minutes timer
+        └── executor.service            system-wide unit (FOUNDER FENCE)
+```
+
+The orchestrator `bin/idp-device-provision` dispatches by `uname -s`:
+
+| Platform | Package mgr | Service mgr | Source files |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| macOS (Darwin) | Homebrew | launchd | `infra/macos/` |
+| Linux | apt | systemd | `infra/linux/` |
+| Windows | winget (planned) | SCM (planned) | dispatched stub |
+
+## Subcommands
+
+```
+bin/idp-device-provision install       lay everything declared + render templates
+bin/idp-device-provision upgrade       upgrade + re-render
+bin/idp-device-provision uninstall     reverses install
+bin/idp-device-provision status        JSON on stdout (consumed by mumchimp.com/devices)
+bin/idp-device-provision doctor        human-readable status + remediation
+bin/idp-device-provision self-test     parses every artifact; CI-runnable on every PR
+```
+
+The status JSON contract is:
+
+```json
+{
+  "platform":  "macos" | "linux",
+  "brew":      {"installed": 0|1, "status": "..."},
+  "apt":       {"installed": 0|1, "status": "..."},
+  "tailscale": {"installed": 0|1, "status": "..."},
+  "spire":     {"installed": 0|1, "status": "..."},
+  "executor":  {"installed": 0|1, "status": "..."},
+  "renew":     {"installed": 0|1, "status": "..."}
+}
+```
+
+`platform` reports the current `uname -s` classification; `brew` is `not_applicable_for_platform`
+on linux and `apt` is `not_applicable_for_platform` on macOS (the shape is stable across
+platforms; only the populated field is relevant to the running OS).
+
+## CI coverage
+
+`.github/workflows/device-provision-test.yml` is the matrix:
+- `macos-14` runner: install → status → uninstall → install (idempotency proven)
+- `ubuntu-24.04` runner: same flow on Linux
+
+`bin/idp-ci` has the `mac-provision` rung that runs on every PR — Brewfile parses,
+plist XML well-formed, systemd unit well-formed, the script parses.
+
+## Bootstrap on a fresh device
+
+The one-time path a new machine:
+
+```bash
+# 1. Homebrew (mac) or apt-get (linux) — already present on every GitHub Actions runner
+#    and most enterprise images. Otherwise install per the standard idiom.
+
+# 2. Clone this repo at the version you want, e.g.
+git clone https://github.com/chidionyema/idp.git /path/to/idp
+
+# 3. Run the orchestrator
+cd /path/to/idp
+bin/idp-device-provision install
+
+# 4. (Founder only) install the executor LaunchDaemon/systemd unit. This step
+#    needs sudo: the orchestrator refuses, by design (FOUNDER FENCE).
+sudo bin/idp-executor-install
+
+# 5. (Any device) one-time per-Mac identity proof -- age identity.
+bin/idp-jit enroll
+
+# 6. Verify
+bin/idp-device-provision doctor
+```
+
+After step 5, the LaunchAgent (Mac) or systemd timer (Linux) renews the
+agent-reader kubeconfig every 10 minutes. The founder never touches
+credentials again on this machine.
+
+## Constraints
+
+- **Per AGENTS.md SPIFFE primary rule 3**: an agent (CI, LLM, IDE) never asks
+  the founder to log in or run a script. The orchestrator IS the one
+  script; everything else is on a timer.
+- **The executor daemon stays a FOUNDER FENCE**: `sudo bin/idp-executor-install`
+  refuses an agent-invoked shell. The executor unit is in `infra/{macos,linux}/`
+  for the founder to lay once per machine, by hand.
+- **No platform-specific config files get committed** — every per-device
+  secret (SPIRE join token, trust bundle, OCI secrets) is fetched from the
+  estate vault at install time, never checked in.
+
+## Adding a new platform
+
+1. Add `infra/<platform>/` with the package list and service manifests
+2. Extend `detect_os` in `bin/idp-device-provision` to classify `uname -s` to your platform
+3. Add `install_<platform>` and `wire_<platform>` functions
+4. Add a CI runner entry to `.github/workflows/device-provision-test.yml`
+5. Add a status row to the `status_json` function (one field per row is fine)
+
+The status JSON contract has stable field order. New platforms add fields; they
+do not reorder or rename.
diff --git a/infra/linux/apt-lists/runtime.txt b/infra/linux/apt-lists/runtime.txt
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..b89bb3056
--- /dev/null
+++ b/infra/linux/apt-lists/runtime.txt
@@ -0,0 +1,57 @@
+# infra/linux/apt-lists/runtime.txt — Debian/Ubuntu packages for `bin/idp-device-provision`
+#
+# Mirrors infra/macos/Brewfile 1:1. Every package here has the same job as
+# its Brewfile row. The provision script reads this file with `xargs -a
+# runtime.txt apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends` after `apt-get update`.
+#
+# Why a plain text list, not a YAML:
+#   # The format above is the exact dpkg / apt-get install-oneliner's own input.
+#   # No parser, no reformat, no "what is the structure". apt-get takes it.
+#   # The CI rung (infra-linux-packages, bin/idp-ci) runs `apt-get install --dry-run
+#   # -y --no-install-recommends - < runtime.txt` on ubuntu-24.04 and FAILS on a
+#   # package that no longer exists in the distro. Same gate model as
+#   # infra-macos-bundle runs `brew bundle check --verbose` on macos-14.
+
+# --- Core CLI tools ---
+jq
+yq
+gh
+shellcheck
+shfmt
+actionlint
+
+# --- Vault / secrets ---
+sops
+age
+
+# --- Kubernetes / GitOps ---
+kubectl
+helm
+flux
+
+# --- SPIFFE / SPIRE primary ---
+# The SPIRE agent binary ships in the official SPIRE apt repo
+# (https://spiffe.io/docs/latest/spire/agents/) — we add that repo at
+# `bin/idp-device-provision install` time and pin via the version in the
+# repo URL, not via the OS package name (no `spire-agent` in distro).
+# apt-mark holds it after install so `apt-get upgrade` doesn't drift the
+# version when we ship a new SPIRE helmrelease.
+
+# --- Tailscale on Linux ---
+# Same as Mac: install via the official apt repo
+# (https://pkgs.tailscale.com/stable/ubuntu/jammy/main) — laid by the
+# provision script, package name `tailscale`. The systemd unit comes from
+# the upstream package, not from infra/linux/systemd-units/, so this row
+# is intentionally absent; the binary + the daemon unit are both handled
+# by the upstream `.deb`.
+
+# --- Mac App Store CLI ---
+# `mas` is Mac-only; absent here. CI matrix excludes it from the Linux rung.
+
+# --- build-from-source rows (no apt package exists) ---
+# oci-cli: same constraint as on macOS — cryptography<46 pin for intel macOS
+# wheels, irrelevant on linux/amd64; the script handles this via the
+# shared `bin/idp-cloud` vendor path.
+# spire-agent: built from the same upstream source via the install_upstream
+# recipe the bash version of the script carries; the version pin lives in
+# `bin/idp-device-provision install` itself, not in this file.
diff --git a/infra/linux/systemd-units/executor.service b/infra/linux/systemd-units/executor.service
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..169be934e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/infra/linux/systemd-units/executor.service
@@ -0,0 +1,69 @@
+# infra/linux/systemd-units/executor.service
+#
+# Gateway-as-floor executor daemon on Linux. The Mac equivalent is the
+# root LaunchDaemon com.idp.executor.plist; the Linux equivalent is a
+# system-wide systemd unit running as the `_idp_executor` uid with its
+# own AF_UNIX socket under /run/idp/executor.sock. The script `bin/idp-
+# executor-install` (already in the repo) is the one place that creates
+# the uid, lays the unit, and chmods the socket; `bin/idp-device-provision
+# install` lays the binary into /usr/local/bin first, then calls that
+# script.
+#
+# Why unit file checked in but install path goes through executor-install:
+# per AGENTS.md SPIFFE primary rule 3, an agent never runs sudo.
+# executor-install itself refuses an agent-invoked shell (FOUNDER FENCE);
+# the founder runs it from their terminal once. This file is the unit
+# the script copies into /etc/systemd/system/.
+
+[Unit]
+Description=Gateway-as-floor executor daemon
+Documentation=bin/idp-executor --help
+After=network-online.target
+Wants=network-online.target
+
+[Service]
+Type=simple
+User=_idp_executor
+Group=_idp_executor
+WorkingDirectory=/var/lib/idp-executor
+ExecStartPre=/usr/bin/install -d -o _idp_executor -g _idp_executor -m 0750 /run/idp
+ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/executor-daemon \
+    --socket /run/idp/executor.sock \
+    --workdir /var/lib/idp-executor \
+    --audit-log /var/log/idp-executor-audit.log
+Restart=on-failure
+RestartSec=5s
+
+# Same hardening as spire-agent. The executor is the only path an agent
+# can write to a protected path; hardening has the same role here as for
+# the SPIRE agent, just one tier down the trust ladder.
+NoNewPrivileges=true
+ProtectSystem=strict
+ProtectHome=read-only
+PrivateTmp=true
+PrivateDevices=true
+ProtectKernelTunables=true
+ProtectKernelModules=true
+ProtectControlGroups=true
+RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX AF_INET AF_INET6
+RestrictNamespaces=true
+RestrictRealtime=true
+SystemCallArchitectures=native
+SystemCallFilter=@system-service
+SystemCallErrorLogs=disabled
+LockPersonality=true
+MemoryDenyWriteExecute=true
+RestrictSUIDSGID=true
+
+RuntimeDirectory=idp
+RuntimeDirectoryMode=0750
+
+# The socket is what every workload (Claude Code, terminals) writes
+# through. Read/write by the executor uid only; everyone else connects
+# without filesystem credentials.
+SocketUser=_idp_executor
+SocketGroup=_idp_executor
+SocketMode=0640
+
+[Install]
+WantedBy=multi-user.target
diff --git a/infra/linux/systemd-units/jit-device-renew.service b/infra/linux/systemd-units/jit-device-renew.service
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..ca3cb07bd
--- /dev/null
+++ b/infra/linux/systemd-units/jit-device-renew.service
@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+# infra/linux/systemd-units/jit-device-renew.service
+#
+# Mirrors com.estate.jit-device-renew.plist on Mac: every 10 minutes,
+# re-mint the agent-reader kubeconfig from the per-device key the broker
+# holds for this machine. The Mac path is a LaunchAgent (per-user, no
+# sudo); the Linux path is `systemd --user` (the per-user systemd
+# instance, installed by `loginctl enable-linger <user>`).
+#
+# Why a Timer pair (this unit + the .timer below) instead of OnCalendar
+# in the Service unit: a Timer unit is what the systemd documentation
+# recommends for periodic jobs, lets multiple Services share one Timer,
+# and keeps `RandomizedDelaySec` honest without anacron.
+
+[Unit]
+Description=Renew agent-reader kubeconfig from the per-device JIT key
+Documentation=bin/idp-jit-device-renew --help
+After=network-online.target
+Wants=network-online.target
+
+[Service]
+Type=oneshot
+User=%i
+Group=%i
+WorkingDirectory=/home/%i
+EnvironmentFile=-/home/%i/.config/spire-renew.env
+ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/idp-jit-device-renew \
+    --quiet \
+    --kubeconfig /home/%i/.local/state/idp/agent.kubeconfig
+
+# Hardening (laxer than the SPIRE agent unit: this unit needs to talk
+# to the broker over the tailnet, which means a real socket; the
+# agent unit instead binds to a Unix socket only).
+NoNewPrivileges=true
+ProtectSystem=full
+ProtectHome=read-only
+PrivateTmp=true
+ProtectKernelTunables=true
+ProtectControlGroups=true
+MemoryDenyWriteExecute=true
+RestrictSUIDSGID=true
+RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX AF_INET AF_INET6
+SystemCallArchitectures=native
+LockPersonality=true
+
+[Install]
+WantedBy=default.target
diff --git a/infra/linux/systemd-units/jit-device-renew.timer b/infra/linux/systemd-units/jit-device-renew.timer
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..b17708cbc
--- /dev/null
+++ b/infra/linux/systemd-units/jit-device-renew.timer
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+# infra/linux/systemd-units/jit-device-renew.timer
+#
+# Activation: every 10 minutes, with up to 30s jitter so a fleet of
+# devices does not stampede the broker at the same boundary.
+# Persistent=true so a missed tick (laptop asleep, daemon down) catches up
+# next time the timer is started.
+#
+# On macOS the same loop runs as a LaunchAgent with StartInterval=600; the
+# math is identical and the renewal threshold (~10m before the 1-hour SVID
+# expiry) holds for both classes of device.
+
+[Unit]
+Description=Renew agent-reader kubeconfig every 10 minutes
+Documentation=bin/idp-jit-device-renew --help
+
+[Timer]
+OnBootSec=2min
+OnUnitActiveSec=10min
+RandomizedDelaySec=30s
+AccuracySec=1s
+Persistent=true
+Unit=git-device-renew.service
+
+[Install]
+WantedBy=timers.target
diff --git a/infra/linux/systemd-units/spire-agent.service b/infra/linux/systemd-units/spire-agent.service
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..a263a8332
--- /dev/null
+++ b/infra/linux/systemd-units/spire-agent.service
@@ -0,0 +1,67 @@
+# infra/linux/systemd-units/spire-agent.service
+#
+# Unit for the SPIRE agent on Linux. Runs as the founder's uid (or the
+# service-account uid that owns the device's age key) under systemd's
+# per-user instance (`systemctl --user`); the system-wide form lands in
+# /etc/systemd/system/ for the root-owned variant.
+#
+# Why a unit file checked into the repo (the script generates no
+# platform-specific id at runtime): the unit's ExecStart path, the
+# Restart policy, and the security sandbox (NoNewPrivileges,
+# ProtectSystem=strict, ProtectHome=read-only) are SPIRE-specific
+# baselines that every device on the estate must agree on. Drift
+# here is a downgrade.
+
+[Unit]
+Description=SPIRE agent -- Workload API + JWT-SVID minting on this device
+Documentation=https://spiffe.io/docs/latest/spire/agent/
+After=network-online.target tailscaled.service
+Wants=network-online.target
+Requires=tailscaled.service
+
+[Service]
+Type=simple
+User=%i
+Group=%i
+WorkingDirectory=/var/lib/spire
+EnvironmentFile=-/etc/spire/agent.conf.env
+ExecStartPre=-/usr/bin/test -S /tmp/spire-agent/public/api.sock
+ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/spire-agent run \
+    -config /etc/spire/agent.conf \
+    -expandEnv
+Restart=on-failure
+RestartSec=5s
+StartLimitInterval=60s
+StartLimitBurst=5
+
+# Hardening. SPIRE is the only path to a JWT-SVID on this device; hardening
+# is the difference between a node that contributes to the trust domain and
+# a node that owns a token-mint surface. The defaults below match what the
+# upstream SPIRE deployment guide ships for unix:uid attestor workloads.
+NoNewPrivileges=true
+ProtectSystem=strict
+ProtectHome=read-only
+PrivateTmp=true
+PrivateDevices=true
+ProtectKernelTunables=true
+ProtectKernelModules=true
+ProtectControlGroups=true
+RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX AF_INET AF_INET6
+RestrictNamespaces=true
+RestrictRealtime=true
+SystemCallArchitectures=native
+SystemCallFilter=@system-service
+SystemCallErrorLogs=disabled
+LockPersonality=true
+MemoryDenyWriteExecute=true
+RestrictSUIDSGID=true
+PrivateUsers=true
+
+# The Workload API socket is what workloads read -- they need write access
+# to a public socket. PrivateTmp=true gives it a fresh /tmp; we bind the
+# socket path explicitly via spire-agent.conf.
+RuntimeDirectory=spire-agent
+RuntimeDirectoryMode=0750
+
+[Install]
+WantedBy=default.target
diff --git a/infra/macos/Brewfile b/infra/macos/Brewfile
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..07af1c1fb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/infra/macos/Brewfile
@@ -0,0 +1,68 @@
+# infra/macos/Brewfile — declarative dependency list for the founder's Mac.
+#
+# Why a Brewfile and not the curl recipes in bin/idp-workstation-bootstrap:
+#
+#   brew bundle install is the enterprise-Mac idiom. It is idempotent (a present
+#   keg is a no-op), it is version-controlled, it survives a clean `git clone` of
+#   this repo, it converges on a fresh Mac in one command, and it is testable in
+#   CI on `macos-14` (where the founder installed Homebrew takes <2 min). The curl
+#   recipes were the opposite of every one of those properties, and the founder's
+#   2026-09-23 "we have memory systems, documents, code yet no one knows how
+#   anything works" was the bill.
+#
+# Idempotency model:
+#
+#   * Every formula is pinned to an exact version. `brew bundle install` will not
+#     upgrade a present keg; out-of-date deps stay pinned until a deliberate
+#     `brew bundle upgrade --formula=X` lands. This makes "what does this Mac
+#     have on it" reproducible from the file.
+#
+#   * The CI rung `infra-macos-bundle` (in bin/idp-ci) runs `brew bundle check
+#     --verbose` after every PR that touches this file. A missing dep is FAIL.
+#
+#   * `brew bundle dump --file infra/macos/Brewfile --force` is the one command
+#     that overwrites this file from the live install; the CI rung will refuse
+#     any PR that runs the same command against an unchanged dep set.
+#
+# Install-from-source rows (spire, tailscale) use the official upstream recipe.
+# Where a stable tap exists (fluxcd/tap/flux) the Brewfile names it explicitly;
+# building from head is never the right default.
+
+# --- Taps ---
+tap "fluxcd/tap"
+
+# --- CLI formulae, pinned ---
+brew "jq",    "1.7.1"
+brew "yq",    "4.40.5"
+brew "gh",    "2.50.0"
+brew "kubectl", "1.30.0"
+brew "helm",  "3.14.0"
+brew "flux",  "2.3.0"   # from fluxcd/tap
+brew "sops",  "3.8.1"
+brew "age",   "1.2.1"
+brew "mas",   "1.8.7"   # Mac App Store CLI for App Store casks
+brew "shellcheck", "0.9.0"
+brew "shfmt",      "3.7.0"
+brew "actionlint", "1.6.27"
+
+# --- SPIRE / SPIFFE primary ---
+# The cluster SPIRE control plane + agent run in the cluster (platform/spire);
+# the Workload API socket + JWT-SVID minting runs on this Mac via this row.
+# Built from the upstream GitHub source because the official homebrew-core
+# formula is the server+agent both; we want the agent pinned to the same
+# version as the cluster's helmrelease.
+brew "spire", "1.15.3"
+
+# --- GUI / system-extension casks ---
+# Tailscale installs the daemon (`tailscaled`) plus the Mac system extension that
+# the OS approves per-machine. The .pkg-install system extension approval is
+# the one Mac GUI prompt in the whole install path; `idp-mac-provision install`
+# invokes `tailscaled` once via launchctl after brew lays the binary, and
+# the user-facing approval happens when the .app is first opened from /Applications.
+cask "tailscale"
+
+# --- Mac App Store ---
+# No MAS apps today. Add here when one ships. `mas signin` once, then
+# `mas install <id>` on every Mac. CI test skips this row because the
+# runner is signed out of MAS.
+# mas "Example App Name", id: 1234567890
diff --git a/infra/macos/LaunchDaemons/com.estate.jit-device-renew.plist b/infra/macos/LaunchDaemons/com.estate.jit-device-renew.plist
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..14020f1fd
--- /dev/null
+++ b/infra/macos/LaunchDaemons/com.estate.jit-device-renew.plist
@@ -0,0 +1,64 @@
+<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
+<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
+<!--
+  com.estate.jit-device-renew.plist — every 10 minutes, re-mint a 1-hour
+  agent-reader kubeconfig from the per-device X25519 key the broker holds for
+  this machine. The original (2026-09-18) commit `5955d76c0`:
+  "the CEO is not a cron job" — the timer path runs a fresh short-lived
+  process every 10 minutes, not a daemon, so a stale state between runs is
+  impossible.
+
+  install path : infra/macos/LaunchDaemons/com.estate.jit-device-renew.plist
+  owner       : the founder (loaded by bin/idp-jit-device-renew install flag)
+  never loaded by an agent session. Per AGENTS.md SPIFFE primary rule 3.
+
+  StartInterval vs StartCalendarInterval:
+  StartInterval at 600s is exact-10-minute, drift-free; the agent-reader
+  SVID has a 1-hour TTL and the renewal threshold is 10 minutes before
+  expiry, so the math is: TTL=3600s, start_interval=600s, renewal_threshold=600s;
+  the kubeconfig is rewritten before its embedded token expires with two
+  intervals of slack.
+-->
+<plist version="1.0">
+<dict>
+    <key>Label</key>
+    <string>com.estate.jit-device-renew</string>
+
+    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
+    <array>
+        <string>/Users/roseonyema/Documents/code/idp/bin/idp-jit-device-renew</string>
+        <string>--quiet</string>
+        <string>--kubeconfig</string>
+        <string>/Users/roseonyema/.local/state/idp/agent.kubeconfig</string>
+    </array>
+
+    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
+    <true/>
+
+    <key>StartInterval</key>
+    <integer>600</integer>
+
+    <key>ProcessType</key>
+    <string>Background</string>
+
+    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
+    <string>/Users/roseonyema/Library/Logs/com.estate.jit-device-renew.out.log</string>
+
+    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
+    <string>/Users/roseonyema/Library/Logs/com.estate.jit-device-renew.err.log</string>
+
+    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
+    <dict>
+        <key>PATH</key>
+        <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>
+        <key>HOME</key>
+        <string>/Users/roseonyema</string>
+    </dict>
+
+    <key>SoftResourceLimits</key>
+    <dict>
+        <key>NumberOfFiles</key>
+        <integer>4096</integer>
+    </dict>
+</dict>
+</plist>
diff --git a/infra/macos/LaunchDaemons/com.idp.executor.plist b/infra/macos/LaunchDaemons/com.idp.executor.plist
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..c71ce5d18
--- /dev/null
+++ b/infra/macos/LaunchDaemons/com.idp.executor.plist
@@ -0,0 +1,89 @@
+<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
+<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
+<!--
+  com.idp.executor.plist — the gateway-as-floor executor daemon. This is a
+  root LaunchDaemon that runs under a uid the agent does not own (_idp_executor)
+  so that an agent which has lost its mind cannot rewrite what answers at
+  ~/.estate/executor.sock. The agent has a separate, owner-only socket; the
+  daemon listens on that socket; the daemon's UID owns the file the agent
+  reads through.
+
+  install path : infra/macos/LaunchDaemons/com.idp.executor.plist
+  owner       : root (loaded by `sudo bin/idp-executor-install`)
+  never loaded by an agent session — per AGENTS.md SPIFFE primary rule 3
+  and per `bin/idp-executor-install`'s own FOUNDER FENCE that refuses an
+  agent-invoked shell.
+
+  Why a separate plist and not an extension of bin/idp-jit-device-renew:
+  the executor daemon and the renew loop have different owner uids, different
+  failure modes (the executor must survive a user logout to keep the gateway
+  enforced; the renew loop cannot run when the user is logged out because
+  it lives in the user's LaunchAgents directory). Two plists, owned at two
+  levels, mirrors the two trust surfaces.
+
+  How `idp-mac-provision install` lays this: the install path emits the
+  rendered plist into /Library/LaunchDaemons with root ownership AFTER
+  `bin/idp-executor-install` has created the _idp_executor uid. If the
+  uid does not exist, the install FAILS — daemon is not loaded, the
+  bootstrap's verify rung reports it as BLIND, and the agent never has
+  a write path that bypasses the gateway.
+-->
+<plist version="1.0">
+<dict>
+    <key>Label</key>
+    <string>com.idp.executor</string>
+
+    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
+    <array>
+        <string>/usr/local/bin/executor-daemon</string>
+        <string>--socket</string>
+        <string>/Users/Shared/.estate/executor.sock</string>
+        <string>--workdir</string>
+        <string>/Users/Shared/.estate</string>
+        <string>--audit-log</string>
+        <string>/var/log/idp-executor-audit.log</string>
+    </array>
+
+    <key>UserName</key>
+    <string>_idp_executor</string>
+
+    <key>GroupName</key>
+    <string>_idp_executor</string>
+
+    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
+    <true/>
+
+    <key>KeepAlive</key>
+    <dict>
+        <key>SuccessfulExit</key>
+        <false/>
+        <key>Crashed</key>
+        <true/>
+        <key>NetworkState</key>
+        <true/>
+    </dict>
+
+    <key>ProcessType</key>
+    <string>Standard</string>
+
+    <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
+    <integer>30</integer>
+
+    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
+    <string>/var/log/com.idp.executor.out.log</string>
+
+    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
+    <string>/var/log/com.idp.executor.err.log</string>
+
+    <key>Sockets</key>
+    <dict>
+        <key>Listener</key>
+        <dict>
+            <key>SockPathName</key>
+            <string>/Users/Shared/.estate/executor.sock</string>
+            <key>SockPathMode</key>
+            <integer>438</integer>
+        </dict>
+    </dict>
+</dict>
+</plist>
